drm/panfrost: select the OPPs for the RK3576 variant

The RK3576 GPU OPP table is written for the plain part, whose top rate is
900 MHz. The RK3576S, J and M stop at 800 MHz, and the J and M want more
voltage at every point than the base part does. Applying this table
unchanged overvolts nothing but does offer those parts a rate they are not
rated for.

Which part this is comes from specification_serial_number in OTP. Panfrost
already has a speed-bin hook, but it passes the cell's value straight to
dev_pm_opp_set_supported_hw(), and Rockchip's cell holds a part number
rather than a bitmask, so the generic path reads it as a nonsense mask.
Give panfrost_compatible a read_speedbin callback for that case and
translate there, matching the vendor's rockchip_get_soc_info().

The cell keeps the binding's "speed-bin" consumer name: it is the cell that
selects the speed bin, and how its contents are encoded is a property of
the SoC, which the per-compatible callback already knows.

An unreadable cell selects a restricted table rather than the base part's,
because the failure mode of guessing is a rate and a voltage the silicon
was not qualified for. The OPP core disables every OPP carrying
opp-supported-hw until supported_hw is set, so this has to run on the
failure path too.

Move specification-serial-number@8 from the Sige5 board dts up into
rk3576.dtsi, next to the other OTP data cells. The GPU node lives in the
SoC dtsi, so a label defined only by one board's dts would not resolve when
any other RK3576 board is built.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

